// Where one character ends and the next begins — UAX #29 extended
// grapheme clusters.
//
// A code point is not a character. `é` may be one code point or two; a
// flag is two; a family emoji is seven, joined by zero-width joiners; and
// `क्षि` is four that a reader sees as one. Every editor that moves the
// cursor, every `truncate` that must not cut an accent off, and every
// column count needs to know where the boundaries are, and none of the
// obvious answers — bytes, code points, `chars()` — is one of them.
//
// ## The rules that are not "look at the next code point"
//
//   - **Regional indicators pair up.** Two make a flag, four make two
//     flags, three make a flag and a spare. So the boundary depends on how
//     many came before, which no single-lookahead rule can see.
//   - **A zero-width joiner glues pictographs.** `👩 ZWJ 💻` is one
//     cluster; `a ZWJ 💻` is two, because the joiner only binds
//     pictographs on both sides.
//   - **An Indic linker binds consonants.** `क` virama `ष` is one cluster,
//     and the virama may have marks around it without breaking the chain.
//     This rule arrived in Unicode 15.1 and is the one most
//     implementations are still missing.
//
// ## Where the tables come from
//
// `GraphemeBreakProperty.txt`, `emoji-data.txt` and the `InCB` values in
// `DerivedCoreProperties.txt`, all for Unicode 17.0.0. The Hangul syllable
// block is left out and computed instead: it is 798 runs of the property
// file and one modulo of arithmetic, which the file's own layout
// guarantees.

/// Triples: first code point, last, and the class number `class_of`
/// answers with. Hangul syllables are not in here — they are 798
/// runs and one line of arithmetic.
